{"author":{"id":"ce771b8a9b8c30a8aac165a8b1cdb0957637d3941276ec4f27335daee16d79de"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794622583,"nonce":"KeBEvvGT4Zgv6uOGRyeBl+aPxiA=","title":"**What to build:** The mechanism, proven end to end on a real page. The backend captures","message":"the Home Assistant user language from the config response it already fetches on every\nreconnect and serves it on a small locale endpoint; the mock Supervisor's config response\ngains a settable `language` field. The frontend integrates the i18n crate with compile-time\ncatalogs (English default, German du-form) seeded with the navigation labels, page\nheadings, and the Grows page, wired through the full locale chain: HA language, then\nbrowser locale, then English. With a German-forced browser, those seeded strings render in\nGerman; everything unseeded stays English — acceptable at this stage.\n\n**Blocked by:** None (can start immediately).\n\n\n- [ ] Backend fetches the HA `language` field alongside the existing config fields and\n      serves it on a locale endpoint\n- [ ] Mock Supervisor config response carries a `language` field (default en, settable)\n- [ ] Frontend catalogs (en + de) exist and compile; en is the key source of truth\n- [ ] Locale chain wired: HA language → browser locale (`de*` → de) → English\n- [ ] Nav labels, page headings, and the Grows page keyed with du-form German\n- [ ] Compile fails when a key is missing from either catalog (parity gate live)\n- [ ] E2e German-forced case: seeded strings render German; default-English assertions pass\n- [ ] Backend integration test: the locale endpoint reflects the mock's config language\n- [ ] The keying pattern is documented for the following batches (one worked example)","files":null}]}